  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1126 AM MDT Fri May 15 2026\n\nTypical spring type pattern over the region through the period. We\nstart out with a mean trough off to the west which has already\nshifted surface winds to the WSW and brought in much drier air.\nWith this trough to our west, winds will be breezy to low end\nwindy into the middle of next week. Expect mainly sunny skies and\nslightly above normal temperatures with the lowlands in the mid\n80s to lower 90s. Very dry air will help overnight lows cool off\ninto the 50s and 60s, it will stay warmer over the eastern slopes\nthe first few days as winds will stay up in the 10-20mph range and\nkeep some low level mixing going. Think the 76 degree low at KELP\nthis morning will be the warmest of the coming days, but wouldn't\nbe surprised to see some areas stay up above 70. Did go above NBM\nguidance for many areas based on keeping some winds and looking at\nlows this morning.\n\nThe upper trough axis pushes east of the area going into midweek.\nAs it does, models all agree on a weak low/trough setting up off\nthe Baja coast. As it does, winds will shift around to the\nsoutheast and push the dryline toward the RGV. Always some\nquestion with the initial westward push with these, but there is\ndecent agreement there will be some storm chances at least in\nHudspeth and Otero counties. Doesn't look like much cooling except\nout in the far east.",
